A||||12||isthiswhereiputausername?||replacing rack & pinion?||||1||
Z||000000||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-06-2001||08:26 PM||||I read some of the topics under search, but still have questions..<P>I am switching the whole rack & tierods to my driver from my parts car, besides removal/installation, and keeping the wheel straight during this, is there anything else I need to look out for?<P><BR>my old one has a lot of play in it, I thought it was my front wheel bearings until I jacked up the front and tried moving the wheel and I see the play is in my rack.. anyone also have any tips on getting mine tight without changing it?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Thanks!<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfiero||11-06-2001||08:32 PM||||What year are we talking about?  The '88s have a passenger side bushing (plastic) that goes bad and causes excessive play, but I'm not sure about the '84-'87s (I don't think they have a bushing).<P>Have you checked your inner and outer tie rods yet?  These go bad and may cause some play too.<P>Even if you keep things straight during the swap you will need to have it realigned (I'm sure you know this, but I figured I would reitterate  it).<P>You may want to consider installing new inner and/or outer tie rods when you do the swap......better now while everything is already apart.  Rodney Dickman sells inner at a steal of a price (maybe outers too?).<P>Good luck either way. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-06-2001||08:43 PM||||Its a 84. <P>I plan on having it realligned since I just changed my rear struts. <P><BR>Thanks<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick86GT||11-06-2001||09:32 PM||||Just take the thing off and take out back: then shoot it!<P>or visit Rodney Dickmans's web site. Buy cheap part and FIX it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||olliver||11-06-2001||10:36 PM||||Trust me man, it's not worth trying to rebuild the rack.  Sure it might cost you $15 dollars to buy the bushings but then you have to pay god only knows how much for the tools to rebuild the darned thing.  And who know's how long it's going to take you.  I asked my friend here who owns his own auto shop, and he says don't even think about it.<P>I just replaced mine cause it had a god awful clunking sound and vibration whenever you drove over any rough surfaces. Turned out to be the passenger side bushing was worn oval.  <BR>Just go out and spend the $180.00 Canadian or $100 American and simply replace the thing.  Trust me you will thank me in the end.  I'm so glad that i replaced my rack NO MORE ANNOYING NOISES!!!!!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||11-07-2001||01:59 AM||||I may be wrong, but I don't think you need any special tools to rebush the rack.  Rodney has many satisfied customers.  I'd definitely try Rodneys fix 1<sup>st</sup> before spending a ton of money on a new rack.  BTW--If you end up buying a new steering rack, save yourself some hassles and buy genuine GM parts.  Autozone racks are generally rebuilt units.  Not always the most reliable.  You can get a brand new rack through GM for less than $200.||||reg||1
Z||000006||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-07-2001||09:01 AM||||I have another good one ready to bolt right back on it. <BR>(Parts car  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)<P>Is there anything I need to look out for when I am changing it to make the job simplier?<P> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000007||DRH||11-07-2001||11:26 AM||||Take the spare tire "tub" out to work on it. The extra room is WELL worth the few minutes it takes to remove it.||||reg||3
Z||000008||isthiswhereiputausername?||11-07-2001||01:13 PM||||I will be doing it over the pit in the garage, should the tub still be taken out?<P>Im hoping to get it done this weekend<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||87fastback||11-11-2001||12:57 PM||||Don't mess with the tub, just yank it out from the bottom.  I did mine in and out in 30 minutes on an 87 GT.  The biggest bummer is getting the steering joint bolt out.  Kind of tight there, but if you just unlock your steering and move it around a little you can get a good angle on it with an 11mm socket with a u-joint and 6" extension.  Kind of contorted, but works.  There are only 4 bolt fastening the bushings plus the tie rod ends.  Once all is loose slid out the drivers side and slide the new one in!  Easiest rack I ever did, especially compared to a 300ZX! yuk!!||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||11-11-2001||01:26 PM||||As indicated above, The rack is no big deal to swap...<P>If I remember right, The pinch bolt in the universal must be removed not simply loosened. Since the universal only fits one way there's no issue with the steering wheel.<P>As worn as you say the rack is,,, check the universal carefully. If it's bad, unfasten the other end under the dash and replace it while things are appart.<P>Yes, You should definatly get it aligned but make sure it doesn't need other things like bushings or ball joints first. <P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||Seanh||11-11-2001||01:44 PM||||Don't know if you know this or not, but also after you removed the pinch bolt, drive a flat screwdriver in the slot to open it up. I struggeled with that for hours because I was so tired I couldn't think right, and then next morning someone told me to put the screwdriver in there.<P>------------------<BR>Sean Heberly - 86 GT<BR>Back to my 3.1L...<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Image2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||artherd||11-11-2001||08:23 PM||||If you have an 88, then get Rodney's bushing. Even if you don't think you need it, you need it!!!!<P>I can't belive the thing that GM put in there...  you won't either when you pull it out. You will wet your pants. (put down plastic.)<P>Anyway, took me half an hour tops (most of that was fscking with the tie rods) and it can quite be done with the rack in the car. <P>Wow, now my car goes straight! what a concept!!!<P>Best!<BR>Ben<BR><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
